der, and only suppress errors when `IgnoreGarbage` is true. For AC3 to work, the test would need to provide a base64 decoder that naturally ignores non-alphabet characters, or the acceptance criteria might just mean that whitespace and standard padding characters are handled gracefully. I'm reconsidering the whole approach—maybe I should try decoding first, and if it fails with IgnoreGarbage enabled, iteratively strip problematic characters and retry until it works or the input is exhausted. But actually, the most practical solution used in real implementations is simpler: strip whitespace, attempt decoding, and if that fails, filter the input through a known-safe character set for that encoding type before retrying. I'm probably overthinking this when I should just check what the actual acceptance criteria are.\n\nLooking at the test requirements, they're straightforward: IgnoreGarbage=true should silently skip non-alphabet characters, while IgnoreGarbage=false should error on invalid input. The implementation doesn't need to be overly clever—I can either handle garbage stripping in the main Decode function or let callers wrap their own Decode logic to handle it.\n\nThe cleaner approach is to treat IgnoreGarbage as a configuration flag that the command layer uses to decide whether to wrap the Decode function with garbage filtering. So in the cmd/ layer, if IgnoreGarbage is true, I'd replace the Decode function with one that strips non-base64 characters before decoding. The core encutil.Decode function stays simple: read input, strip whitespace, call cfg.Decode, return the result. This follows the Strategy pattern and keeps concerns separated.\n\nBut wait—the spec says the Decode function itself must strip non-alphabet characters when IgnoreGarbage is true. The problem is the function doesn't know what the alphabet is without being told. For Base64 it's A-Za-z0-9+/=, for Base32 it's A-Z2-7=. The DecoderConfig doesn't have an alphabet field, so how can the function k